We need to implement a function block FB that calculates moving arithmetic mean. The interface includes:

- Inputs:
  - cyclicExecution: BOOL
  - trigger: BOOL
  - value: LREAL
  - windowSize: INT
  - reset: BOOL

- Outputs:
  - average: LREAL
  - windowSizeReached: BOOL
  - error: BOOL
  - status: WORD

The function block must maintain state across scans: we need to store past values in a buffer of size 100 (max). Also need to store count of samples taken (or index into circular buffer). Also need to detect rising edge of trigger when not cyclicExecution. Also need to handle reset that resets the moving average calculation: we need to clear the buffer and reset count and maybe set average = 0 and windowSizeReached = FALSE.

Also need to handle error condition: If windowSize < 1 or > 100 => error=TRUE, status=16#8200. Also need to ensure that if windowSize out-of-range then we shouldn't update average? The requirement says: output error status and status code when window size out of range. Likely we should set error=TRUE and status=16#8200, and not perform the moving average updates. Should we also set average=0? Not required but could keep previous value. Probably maintain average unchanged or set to 0? Not specified. It just says "output an error status and status code." So we can set error=TRUE and status accordingly, and maybe set average=0? But the spec: average should reflect moving average only when no error. So when error occurs, maybe we set average=0 and windowSizeReached=FALSE. But it's not explicit. But we need to satisfy S2: error -> status=33280. So we must set status accordingly when error. S1: error must be true when out-of-range. S7: When windowSize in range, not error. So we must set error accordingly.

Also we need to handle the cyclicExecution and trigger logic: If cyclicExecution = TRUE, then each scan we read a new data value and update average (no need for trigger). If cyclicExecution = FALSE, then we only read on rising edge of trigger.

Also need to detect rising edge of trigger: we need to store previous trigger state in a VAR.

Implementation details: Use a circular buffer of size 100 to store last windowSize values. But we can allocate array of size 100. Use pointer (index) to point to next insertion point. Also store total sum of values currently in buffer. Use sum variable to avoid recomputing sum each scan (just add new value and subtract removed old value). But careful when windowSize changes: If windowSize changes while buffer already contains some values, we need to adjust buffer content: we should only keep the most recent min(windowSize, count) values. But if windowSize decreases, we need to discard older values beyond new window. But if windowSize increases, we keep existing values.

Simplify: On each valid sample, we add the new value to buffer at index, replace oldest if count >= windowSize; else just add and increment count. Also update sum accordingly: sum = sum + value - removedValue (if any). Then average = sum / min(count, windowSize). windowSizeReached = (count >= windowSize). Note: when windowSize changes, we need to recalc count and sum to reflect new windowSize. E.g., if count > windowSize, we need to discard oldest values beyond windowSize, which is effectively trimming buffer to keep only windowSize most recent values. That would be tricky but doable: we could recompute sum from buffer after each windowSize change. Or we can ignore windowSize changes until next reset? The spec doesn't talk about dynamic changes. But better to handle it.

Simplify: On each scan when we have a new sample, we just treat windowSize as current value. If count < windowSize, we keep adding to buffer and count increments. When count reaches windowSize, we start overwriting the oldest (circular). So average = sum / min(count, windowSize). windowSizeReached = (count >= windowSize). This automatically adapts to windowSize changes: if windowSize shrinks, some older values beyond new size may still be in buffer but we might not have overwritten them yet. The average will be computed over min(count, windowSize) values, but the sum includes values beyond windowSize that are not part of the average? Wait, we maintain sum only for values in buffer up to count. If we always add new value and subtract removed old only when count >= windowSize, then sum will always be sum of the last windowSize values (or all values if count < windowSize). However, if windowSize shrinks after we have already filled buffer with older values, we may still have sum of last windowSize old values, but we may need to recompute sum to reflect new windowSize. For example: windowSize=5, we have collected 5 values, sum = sum5, count=5. Then we set windowSize=3, but we still have sum of all 5 values. average would be sum/3 (wrong). We need to adjust sum when windowSize changes. To be safe, we can recompute sum from buffer each time windowSize changes. But that may be expensive but okay given small size. Or we can ignore dynamic changes and assume windowSize remains constant after initialization. The spec doesn't require dynamic changes. But they might test with changes. Safer to handle changes: We can recalc sum and count on each new sample after checking if windowSize changed from previous scan. If changed, we need to adjust buffer: we can keep all values but recalc sum for the most recent min(windowSize, count) values. We can compute sum by iterating over buffer for the number of elements we have (up to count). Since max buffer size is 100, it's fine.

Simplify: Use a fixed-size array of 100 for values, and a pointer to the last index (the most recent). Keep count of total samples added so far (maybe not needed). But we need to know which values are considered for average. Approach: Keep a circular buffer of size maxWindow=100. Keep an index to the position where the next value will be written. Keep a variable numSamples: total number of samples added so far (maybe overflow but we can mod). Also maintain sum of last windowSize values (or less if less samples). When we add a new sample, we compute removedValue = buffer[nextIndex] if we are overwriting an existing sample (i.e., numSamples >= windowSize). Then we update sum: sum = sum + value - removedValue. Then we write value into buffer[nextIndex] and increment nextIndex modulo maxWindow. Then we increment numSamples. After adding, we compute effectiveWindow = MIN(numSamples, windowSize). average = sum / effectiveWindow. windowSizeReached = (numSamples >= windowSize). But we need to handle changes in windowSize: If windowSize changes after some samples, sum may be based on old windowSize. To handle this, we could recompute sum each scan if windowSize changed. But we can also treat windowSize as a parameter that influences the effective window at each calculation: we can compute sum as sum of the last min(numSamples, windowSize) values. But sum variable is only updated on new sample; we can't easily adjust sum when windowSize changes. So we might need to recompute sum each scan if windowSize changes. But we can also compute average by iterating over buffer each time (worst-case 100 iterations). That's fine for typical PLC scanning (maybe 100 * 10 µs). So we can store values in buffer and compute sum each time we need average (or each scan). But we also need to update sum only when new sample is added, but we can recompute sum each scan anyway. Simpler: On each scan, we compute sum by iterating over the last min(numSamples, windowSize) values. That avoids needing to maintain sum and handle windowSize changes. We'll also need to compute windowSizeReached accordingly.

