VAR
    // buffer for values
    buffer: ARRAY[0..99] OF LREAL;
    // index of the latest value (most recent)
    idx: INT := 0;
    // total number of samples added
    totalCount: INT := 0;
    // previous trigger state for edge detection
    prevTrigger: BOOL := FALSE;
    // previous windowSize to detect changes (optional)
    prevWindowSize: INT := 0;
END_VAR

But we also need to handle reset: When reset = TRUE, we should reset totalCount = 0, idx = 0, average = 0.0, windowSizeReached = FALSE. Also maybe clear buffer? Not needed but we can set idx = 0 and totalCount = 0.

Now need to compute average each scan when not error. But we need to decide when to compute average: always compute after possible new sample, if no error. If error (windowSize out-of-range), set error=TRUE and status=16#8200, and maybe set average unchanged? But spec doesn't require to set average to any particular value. But we can set average = 0. Or we can keep previous. Safer to set average = 0 and windowSizeReached = FALSE. But S4 says reset -> average = 0. That's for reset, not error. So not required for error. But we may want to set average = 0 for safety. However, we must also ensure that status is set correctly: error -> status = 33280; no error -> status = 0.

Now, we also need to handle the case when cyclicExecution = TRUE and trigger is irrelevant. But we also need to handle rising edge detection for trigger only when cyclicExecution = FALSE. So we need to detect rising edge only in that case. If cyclicExecution = TRUE, we ignore trigger.

Thus logic:

Each scan:
1. If reset = TRUE then perform reset: set totalCount = 0, idx = 0, buffer contents maybe set to 0? Not needed but we can fill with zeros. Also average = 0.0, windowSizeReached = FALSE, error = FALSE, status = 0. Also set prevTrigger = FALSE? Not needed but we can reset. Then exit early (or continue). The spec says "reset signal resets the moving average calculation when the reset signal is TRUE." So we should reset everything and not process new sample on same scan? Probably we should reset first and then maybe ignore other inputs. But it's ambiguous. Usually reset should be processed first and then other logic. We can treat reset as highest priority: if reset = TRUE then we reset and skip any updates for that scan. That satisfies S4 and S5.

2. Check windowSize error: If windowSize < 1 OR windowSize > 100 then error = TRUE; status = 16#8200; maybe set average unchanged or zero; windowSizeReached = FALSE? Not required but we can set windowSizeReached = FALSE. Then exit early (skip reading new data). However, we need to also consider that if reset is TRUE and windowSize out-of-range, what to do? Probably reset overrides error. But spec doesn't mention that. We'll treat reset first: if reset then we reset and exit, ignoring error condition. So error condition only checked if reset is FALSE.

Thus algorithm:

```
IF reset THEN
    // Reset everything
    totalCount := 0;
    idx := 0;
    // Optionally clear buffer
    FOR i := 0 TO 99 DO
        buffer[i] := 0.0;
    END_FOR;
    average := 0.0;
    windowSizeReached := FALSE;
    error := FALSE;
    status := 16#0000;
    prevTrigger := FALSE;
    // exit early: skip reading new sample
    RETURN;
END_IF;

// Check window size error
IF windowSize < 1 OR windowSize > 100 THEN
    error := TRUE;
    status := 16#8200;
    // maybe set average = 0? but not required
    windowSizeReached := FALSE;
    // No new data processing
    RETURN;
END_IF;

// If we reach here, windowSize is valid
error := FALSE;
status := 16#0000;

// Determine if we need to read a new value
// Condition: (cyclicExecution) OR (trigger rising edge)
VAR
    readNew: BOOL;
END_VAR
readNew := FALSE;
IF cyclicExecution THEN
    readNew := TRUE;
ELSE
    // detect rising edge of trigger
    IF trigger AND NOT prevTrigger THEN
        readNew := TRUE;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

// Update prevTrigger
prevTrigger := trigger;

// If readNew, add new value to buffer
IF readNew THEN
    // Write value into buffer at idx
    buffer[idx] := value;
    idx := idx + 1;
    IF idx >= 100 THEN idx := 0; END_IF; // but buffer size is 100, but windowSize max 100, we can handle up to 100
    // Increment totalCount, but cap at 100 (or maybe we don't need cap)
    IF totalCount < 100 THEN totalCount := totalCount + 1; END_IF;
    // Note: If totalCount > windowSize, we will later compute average over windowSize.
END_IF;

// Compute average: sum of most recent min(totalCount, windowSize) values.
VAR
    effectiveCount: INT;
    sum: LREAL;
    i: INT;
END_VAR
effectiveCount := MIN(totalCount, windowSize);
sum := 0.0;
FOR i := 0 TO effectiveCount - 1 DO
    // We need to get the i-th most recent value from buffer.
    // idx points to next write position (i.e., one past the most recent).
    // So the most recent value is at (idx - 1) mod 100.
    // Then the i-th previous is at (idx - 1 - i) mod 100.
    // Compute index:
    VAR idx2: INT;
    idx2 := (idx - 1 - i) % 100;
    // In IEC 61131-3, MOD of negative numbers may be negative, so we need to adjust.
    // Better to compute as: idx2 := (idx - 1 - i);
    // if idx2 < 0 then idx2 := idx2 + 100;
    // Then sum := sum + buffer[idx2];
END_FOR;

// Actually we can compute sum in a loop from i = 0 to effectiveCount-1:
sum := 0.0;
FOR i := 0 TO effectiveCount - 1 DO
    VAR idx2: INT;
    idx2 := idx - 1 - i;
    IF idx2 < 0 THEN idx2 := idx2 + 100; END_IF;
    sum := sum + buffer[idx2];
END_FOR;

average := sum / LREAL(effectiveCount);

// Determine windowSizeReached:
windowSizeReached := (totalCount >= windowSize);
```

But we also need to handle the case when effectiveCount = 0 (i.e., no samples yet). In that case average should be 0.0 (division by zero not allowed). So we should check if effectiveCount > 0 before dividing. If effectiveCount = 0, set average = 0.0.

Now we need to ensure we handle the condition that windowSizeReached should be set correctly for windowSize=1 case when we have a new sample. The spec S8: "尺寸一且此拍有有效采样时必须宣告满窗." Means: When windowSize = 1 and there is a valid sample in this scan (i.e., cyclicExecution or rising trigger), then windowSizeReached must be TRUE. Our logic sets windowSizeReached = (totalCount >= windowSize). After we add a new sample, totalCount becomes at least 1, so for windowSize=1, totalCount >= 1 => TRUE. So it satisfies S8.

